#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173097} :activity/id #uuid "4e3e11c5-f10e-59a8-a6b4-34e2c5e85cc6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050142 :topic/id #uuid "30510c62-b4f5-5b4d-9d00-f64786d1c9a9" :topic/math-academy-id 3546} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173174 :tutorial/content "Recall that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the parabola's vertex.\n\nIf we are given the equation of a parabola in standard form, we can obtain its vertex form by completing the square.\n\nFor example, consider the parabola\n\n$$\ny= x^2+6x+16\n$$\n\nTo express the equation of this parabola in vertex form, we complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{2} + 6x + 16 \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 16 \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2} - 3^{2} + 16 \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2}) - 3^{2} + 16 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2} - 3^{2} + 16 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2} - 9 + 16 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2} + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressed in vertex form, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (x - (-3))^{2} + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex of the parabola is at the point $({\\color{blue}-3},{\\color{red}7})$." :tutorial/id #uuid "98a78c68-d6fd-5642-a60b-1ac9e65d17ac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5186 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "19378221-e834-5c4b-990e-07c0375a2e82" :step/math-academy-id 458344 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172136 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171826 :question/id #uuid "6b2a8d64-5e70-598c-8baa-f7eb97ed0daf" :question/math-academy-id "e-7486"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b042103-9e24-53d9-bc20-80a9a90d191b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050372}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172479}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Parabola in Vertex Form"} :step/id #uuid "51e398bc-e128-5132-8544-f247f071eb64" :step/math-academy-id 458345 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173099}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172137 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171743 :question/id #uuid "108ee1b6-2266-5956-b06d-4f4092fdee57" :question/math-academy-id "e-1469"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a23a10c1-a091-55bb-98a6-7718e46d841b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521465}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Parabola With Leading Coefficients in Vertex Form"} :step/id #uuid "7c8b71e0-5a17-55a7-a089-43d023c422de" :step/math-academy-id 458349 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172138 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171825 :question/id #uuid "0a48df2c-c48e-5862-884f-3768abfbe5df" :question/math-academy-id "e-7478"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d374538-dedf-5a73-9cec-d30491eb1393" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521541}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Parabola Given Its Vertex and Leading Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "cf650db5-7716-5a21-8630-34c4a846767d" :step/math-academy-id 458352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173101}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173101 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173281 :tutorial/content "**Note**: You may find the algebra in this section a little tricky. It's a good idea to go through it, but you will not be quizzed on it. You may skip this section if you wish.\n\n![](images/2a/2a400b12675f8ebf19ba563192d9ae2296336e40ea380d40e6247564718a2b1f.png)\n\nYou may have seen already that for the parabola\n\n$$\ny=ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\nthe **axis of symmetry** of this parabola is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to derive this formula.\n\nSince\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe can take out a factor of $a$ from the right-hand side in the parabola formula:\n\n$$\ny = a\\left(x^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x + \\dfrac{c}{a} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}x + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2}_{\\textrm{perfect square}} - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 - \\dfrac{b^2-4ac}{4a^2} = \\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 + \\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a^2}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na((x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a^{2}}) &= a(x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + a \\cdot \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a^{2}} \\\\\n&= a(x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a} \\\\\n&= a(x - (-\\frac{b}{2a})_(⏟)_(h))^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a}_(⏟)_(k)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy denoting\n\n$$\nh=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}, \\qquad k=\\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a}\n$$\n\nwe get the equation of the parabola in vertex form:\n\n$$\ny = a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere the vertex is given by\n\n$$\n(h,k) = \\left(-\\dfrac{b}{2a}, \\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a} \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, since the parabola's axis of symmetry is the vertical line passing through the vertex, the equation of the axis must be\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "41e62e06-e589-55dc-846e-1177555ce582" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9116 :tutorial/title "Derivation of the Axis of Symmetry Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7ee3f8d6-f452-5eee-97bb-9be3735604ff" :step/math-academy-id 458355}] :activity/title "Writing the Equation of a Parabola in Vertex Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172214}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521636} :activity/id #uuid "fc0fce43-4499-5dbf-871b-021534ffbc9a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050142 :topic/id #uuid "30510c62-b4f5-5b4d-9d00-f64786d1c9a9" :topic/math-academy-id 3546} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c5f552-e5b6-5559-a765-f234acd45481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+12x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520769} {:answer/id #uuid "64f46c4f-ef14-508b-ba0b-1d70cbde1e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+10x+36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520992} {:answer/id #uuid "7b334842-d776-5002-89d1-297f60f2ce75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+16x+44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521041} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9df875-09de-5c17-832a-6e4fe1c917ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+14x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521223} {:answer/id #uuid "e4344725-2489-5734-9f72-331c833ba4d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+8x+22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521276}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521041} :answer-field/id #uuid "89f78a2b-38d6-57b1-bd43-e097413569f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b1cfb6c9-bb07-5ff5-8e41-4388a89b7e12" :question/math-academy-id "q-106267" :question/problem "Find the equation of the parabola whose vertex is at $(-8,-20)$ and whose leading coefficient is $1.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola, and $a$ is the leading coefficient.\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-8,-20)$ and $a=1$ into the above and then expanding and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1(x-(-8))^{2}-20 \\\\  & =(x+8)^{2}-20 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+16x+64-20 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+16x+44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is $y={x}^{2}+16x+44.$"} :step/id #uuid "11ddce49-fc64-55d5-b99d-ac3a1e4e7463"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd5a798-2c08-573f-b6a3-6bcc3b671500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+12x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520787} {:answer/id #uuid "0f860513-b831-5135-a97b-6bb00ddca02d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+12x+18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520796} {:answer/id #uuid "2359fbce-83d6-54b9-89ea-c9e3b3715a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+10x+36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520837} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7dbffc-f213-5b47-90bd-daf4ab1c319a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+20x+54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521055} {:answer/id #uuid "cefc0cc3-384d-5cca-acd1-5118bce61b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+16x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521231}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521055} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d3f167d-1c36-5660-a850-11a37b74e56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39734cdd-98b1-5bc5-a3ba-0e209592784a" :question/math-academy-id "q-13411" :question/problem "Find the equation of the parabola whose vertex is $(-5,4)$ and leading coefficient is $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola, and $a$ is the leading coefficient.\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-5,4)$ and $a=2$ into the above and then expanding and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(x-(-5))^{2}+4 \\\\  & =2(x+5)^{2}+4 \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+10x+25)+4 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+20x+50+4 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+20x+54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is $y=2{x}^{2}+20x+54.$"} :step/id #uuid "b6cc5e9a-8556-5a56-8111-da471df13050" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044741b5-1377-52d6-ad6f-88da086986f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2(x+2)^{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520770} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa27a7f-66c9-5c3c-a1c6-570268e0230b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2(x-2)^{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520855} {:answer/id #uuid "530f0edf-0df9-5cdb-8936-b5d96998e8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2(x+2)^{2}-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520952} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a97644-8ca9-5dc3-82d6-86d67ef9b796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2(x+2)^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521126} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1e6118-37dd-5e5e-9039-29e80ac12e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2(x-2)^{2}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520770} :answer-field/id #uuid "be2e5806-e4f1-5967-90f4-2ffdb0fd7652" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1b44bc96-863e-5a73-a2ed-628d5188e295" :question/math-academy-id "q-53700" :question/problem "Write the equation of the parabola $y=-2{x}^{2}-8x-14$ in vertex form." :question/worked-solution "To express the equation of the parabola in vertex form, we need to complete the square. \n\nFirst, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}-8x-14=-2({x}^{2}+4x+7)\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+7 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+7 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2})-{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ (x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ (x+2)^{2}-4+7 & = \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+4x+7) & = \\\\ -2((x+2)^{2}+3) & = \\\\ -2(x+2)^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressed in vertex form, the equation of the parabola is $y=-2(x+2)^{2}-6.$"} :step/id #uuid "f5d9c43c-614d-5288-9746-7f31a2326b3e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521577}}] :activity/title "Writing the Equation of a Parabola in Vertex Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520761}]}
